# SYNOPSIS

```usage
ls [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

For each directory argument, `ls` prints the names in the directory, sorted by their bytes, on one line, two spaces apart, then a newline; an empty directory prints an empty line.
For each other <file> it prints the argument as given, on a line of its own.
With no <file> it lists `/`: there is no current directory.

Each <file> is looked up in the namespace `ls` was started with, and must be an absolute path.
A directory that is a union (see ns(1)) is read as each member in turn, and every member's names are listed.

`ls` takes no flags: an argument beginning with `-` is a file's name.
It prints names only, never a file's mode, size, owner or time, and never quotes a name.
It lists the first 512 names of a directory, holding at most 8192 bytes of them; the rest are left out.

In the POSIX namespace `ls` is sbase's command instead (see {The POSIX command|#posix}).

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% ls /
adm  bin  boot  dev  dist  lib  n  net  proc  rc  srv  sys  tmp
% ls /nothing | tail -1; if(~ $status 'ls '*': error|') echo status ok
ls: /nothing: file does not exist
status ok
```

# SEE ALSO

ns(1), rc(1)

# DIAGNOSTICS

When a <file> cannot be found, `ls` says so on standard error, as `ls: /nothing: file does not exist`, and goes on to the next.
If any <file> fails the exit string is `error`; it is `no namespace` when `ls` was started without one.

# BUGS

A relative path is refused as `bad message`, the error a malformed path is given.

A directory found but not opened, or not read to its end, fails with no message; what was read of it is still printed.

Names past the 512th, or past 8192 bytes, are dropped without a word.
A name in two members of a union is listed twice.

@node=posix title="The POSIX command"

```text
ls [-1AacdFfHhiLlnpqRrSUu] [file ...]
```

In the POSIX namespace (a manifest's `ns=posix`, made by `/lib/ns/posix`, see namespace(6)), `/boot/bin/posix` is bound before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so `ls` runs sbase's command, as POSIX specifies.
It lists each <file> given, and the contents of each directory; with none it lists the current directory.
Names are sorted and printed one to a line; with more than one argument each directory's listing is headed by its name.

: `-A`
  List names beginning with `.` too, but not `.` and `..` themselves.
: `-a`
  List names beginning with `.` too.
: `-c`, `-u`
  Use the time of the last status change, or of the last access, instead of the last modification, to sort or print.
: `-d`
  List a directory itself, not its contents.
: `-F`, `-p`
  Append a mark of the file's type to every special file, or `/` to directories only.
: `-f`
  As `-U` with `-a`, and with `-r`, `-S` and `-t` turned off.
: `-H`, `-L`
  Follow symbolic links named as arguments, or all symbolic links.
: `-h`
  Print sizes in human-readable units.
: `-i`
  Print each file's index number.
: `-l`, `-n`
  List the type, permissions, links, owner, group, size and time of each file; `-n` prints the owner and group as numbers.
: `-q`, `-1`
  Print `?` for each unprintable character of a name.
: `-R`
  List directories recursively.
: `-r`
  Reverse the order.
: `-S`, `-t`, `-U`
  Sort by size, largest first, or by time, latest first; or do not sort.

sbase's page is `third_party/sbase/ls.1` in the repository.
